  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "For what real values of \\(K\\) does \\(x=K^2(x-1)(x-2)\\) have real roots?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "none"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(-2\\lt K\\lt1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(-2\\sqrt2\\lt K\\lt2\\sqrt2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(K\\gt1\\) or \\(K\\lt-2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "all"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1962-p34.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Rearranging gives \\[ K^2x^2-(3K^2+1)x+2K^2=0. \\] Its discriminant is \\[ \\begin{aligned} &(3K^2+1)^2-8K^4\\\\ &\\qquad=K^4+6K^2+1\\gt0 \\end{aligned} \\] for every real \\(K.\\) This also covers \\(K=0,\\) when the original equation gives \\(x=0.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Expand and collect terms to obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\)",
        "Its discriminant simplifies to \\(K^4+6K^2+1\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
